(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-7-sleep-apnea-ohs-vignettes-version-2"]={"title":"Obstructive Sleep Apnea and Obesity Hypoventilation Vignettes 2 — CMS I Exam 7","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 7","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A 57-year-old man with newly diagnosed obstructive sleep apnea drinks several glasses of wine each night and takes an over-the-counter sedative to fall asleep. Which advice addresses these modifiable risk factors?","choices":["Avoid alcohol and other sedatives","Keep the sedative, stop the wine","Keep the wine, stop the sedative","Take both earlier in the evening"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Use of alcohol or sedatives is a risk factor for obstructive sleep apnea, and the risk modifications are to avoid alcohol and other sedatives, along with diet and exercise for weight loss. Both his nightly wine and his sleep aid fall under that advice. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 6, 15"},{"q":"A 51-year-old man is newly diagnosed with obesity hypoventilation syndrome and coexisting obstructive sleep apnea. Which referral is part of his patient education and care?","choices":["Thoracic surgeon for tracheostomy","No referral needed","Sleep specialist","Bariatric surgeon first"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Patient education for obesity hypoventilation syndrome includes referral to a sleep specialist with proper counseling, and management needs a multidisciplinary approach. First line is a weight loss program with continuous positive airway pressure, the device for the syndrome with obstructive sleep apnea. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 34, 33"},{"q":"A 46-year-old man has habitual snoring and daytime sleepiness. During 6 hours of sleep his polysomnogram records 48 apneas, 42 hypopneas and 36 respiratory effort-related arousals, predominantly obstructive. What is his apnea-hypopnea index?","choices":["7 events per hour","8 events per hour","15 events per hour","21 events per hour"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The apnea-hypopnea index is (apneas + hypopneas) divided by total sleep time in hours: (48 + 42) / 6 = 90 / 6 = 15 events per hour. Respiratory effort-related arousals belong to the respiratory disturbance index, not to this one. At 15 or more predominantly obstructive events, the diagnosis is confirmed. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"A 50-year-old woman reports loud snoring and feeling tired most days. No one has seen her stop breathing during sleep, and she is not treated for high blood pressure. Her body mass index is 38 kg/m2 and her neck (shirt collar) measures 15 inches. What is her STOP-Bang (snoring, tiredness, observed apnea, pressure, body mass index, age, neck, gender) score?","choices":["Score of 2","Score of 3","Score of 4","Score of 5"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Snoring 1 + tired 1 + observed apnea 0 + pressure 0 + body mass index 38 (more than 35) 1 + age 50 (not older than 50) 0 + neck 15 inches 0 + female 0 = 3. The count alone is intermediate (3 to 4), and her 2 yes answers to the first 4 questions plus a body mass index above 35 meet the questionnaire's added high-risk rule. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 16, 17"},{"q":"A 53-year-old man has loud snoring, witnessed apneas and daytime sleepiness, and his primary care clinician suspects obstructive sleep apnea. Which referral is part of his care?","choices":["Cardiologist","Sleep physician","Bariatric surgeon","Maxillofacial surgeon"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Patient education for obstructive sleep apnea includes referral to a sleep physician, together with proper counseling. His snoring, witnessed apneas and sleepiness make the diagnosis likely, and confirming it takes a sleep study. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 15, 13, 18"},{"q":"A 48-year-old man has loud, habitual snoring, unrefreshing sleep, morning headaches and nocturia. His wife has seen him stop breathing and wake gasping. His blood pressure is poorly controlled on two medicines. His neck circumference is 18 inches, and his posterior oropharynx is crowded. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Narcolepsy","Obstructive sleep apnea","Central sleep apnea","Obesity hypoventilation syndrome"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Loud habitual snoring, witnessed apneas, awakening with gasping, unrefreshing sleep, morning headaches, nocturia and poorly controlled hypertension are its history findings, and a large neck and a crowded posterior oropharynx are its examination findings. Narrowing of the upper airway by soft tissue or structure causes it. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 5, 7, 10, 24, 25, 32"},{"q":"A 66-year-old woman with a body mass index of 42 kg/m2 has obesity hypoventilation syndrome, confirmed by exclusion. Her husband has witnessed few apneas, and polysomnography with continuous carbon dioxide monitoring shows sleep-related hypoventilation. Which first-line treatment fits her?","choices":["Tracheostomy with nocturnal ventilation","Bariatric surgery as first therapy","Weight loss plus bilevel positive airway pressure","Weight loss plus continuous positive airway pressure"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. First-line therapy for obesity hypoventilation syndrome is a weight loss program with positive airway pressure, and the device follows the overlap: with sleep-related hypoventilation it is bilevel positive airway pressure. Her few witnessed apneas, older age and female sex fit that 10% subgroup. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 33, 30, 32"},{"q":"A 52-year-old man's polysomnogram shows repeated events in which thermistor airflow falls by 90% or more for 15 seconds while chest and abdominal respiratory effort continue. How is each event classified?","choices":["Central apnea","Mixed apnea","Hypopnea","Obstructive apnea"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. An apnea is a 90% or greater fall in airflow on the thermistor lasting at least 10 seconds, and when airflow stops but respiratory effort continues, the apnea is obstructive. The airway collapses while the patient still tries to breathe, the event pattern of obstructive sleep apnea. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 3, 24"},{"q":"A 64-year-old man with obesity hypoventilation syndrome asks what most often causes death in people with his condition. What should he be told?","choices":["Hypercapnic respiratory failure","Motor vehicle crashes","Complications of diabetes","Cardiovascular disease"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The leading cause of death in obesity hypoventilation syndrome is cardiovascular disease. Untreated, the syndrome tends to progress, and many patients develop pulmonary hypertension and right-sided heart failure; mortality and morbidity are high. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 35, 30, 34, 18"},{"q":"A 70-year-old man with heart failure reports insomnia, frequent awakenings and nocturnal dyspnea. His wife says he rarely snores, and his breathing at night shows Cheyne-Stokes breathing (hyperventilation alternating with apnea). What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Obesity hypoventilation syndrome","Panic attacks","Central sleep apnea","Obstructive sleep apnea"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Heart failure is a risk factor for central sleep apnea, especially through Cheyne-Stokes breathing, hyperventilation alternating with apnea from increased sensitivity to carbon dioxide and a prolonged circulation delay to the carotid chemoreceptors. Insomnia, awakenings and nocturnal dyspnea fit, and snoring is less common than in obstructive apnea. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 24, 25, 10, 32"},{"q":"A 44-year-old man with obstructive sleep apnea has respiratory events on polysomnogram that occur when he sleeps on his back and not when he lies on his side. He wants a measure to add to his care. Which therapy targets this pattern?","choices":["Positional therapy","Nasal steroid spray","Palatal implants","Tracheostomy"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Positional therapy fits when events occur in the supine position: a sleep belt or sleep shirt keeps him lying on his side. His events are confined to back-sleeping, the exact pattern it addresses. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 12, 13"},{"q":"A 52-year-old woman with loud snoring and fatigue has an 8-hour polysomnogram that records 40 apneas, 32 hypopneas and 40 respiratory effort-related arousals. What is her respiratory disturbance index?","choices":["4 events per hour","5 events per hour","9 events per hour","14 events per hour"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The respiratory disturbance index is (apneas + hypopneas + respiratory effort-related arousals) divided by total sleep time in hours: (40 + 32 + 40) / 8 = 112 / 8 = 14 events per hour. The arousals are obstructive airflow reductions that are neither apneas nor hypopneas. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"A 55-year-old man with long-standing untreated obstructive sleep apnea has blood tests drawn. Which laboratory change is expected from his untreated disease?","choices":["Increased C-reactive protein","Increased growth hormone","Increased prolactin","Decreased cortisol"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The endocrine effects of untreated obstructive sleep apnea include increased C-reactive protein, cortisol and amyloid, with decreased growth hormone and prolactin. Proinflammatory cytokines also rise, and they improve with continuous positive airway pressure. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 21, 22"},{"q":"A 48-year-old woman with newly diagnosed obesity hypoventilation syndrome asks to start a weight-loss medication instead of a diet and exercise program. What should she be told?","choices":["Medication follows failed diet and exercise","Medication is the first-line therapy","Medication can replace pressure therapy","Surgery must come before medication"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Medications for obesity hypoventilation syndrome are reserved for those who fail diet and exercise measures. First line is a weight loss program with lifestyle changes (diet, exercise, behavioral modification) plus positive airway pressure, within a multidisciplinary approach. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"A 52-year-old man who takes long-term opioid medications has central sleep apnea confirmed on polysomnography. He has no heart failure and lives at sea level. Which step treats the underlying cause?","choices":["Start adaptive servo-ventilation","Optimize heart failure therapy","Add a stimulant for sleepiness","Reduce the opioids"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Central sleep apnea is treated first by addressing the underlying cause, and when the patient takes opioid medications, the step is to reduce the opioids. After that, continuous positive airway pressure is considered, and adaptive servo-ventilation if it fails. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 26, 24, 11"},{"q":"A 60-year-old man has central sleep apnea after a stroke. His heart function is normal, he takes no opioids and he lives at sea level. A trial of continuous positive airway pressure has failed. What is the next therapy to consider?","choices":["Bilevel positive airway pressure","Adaptive servo-ventilation","Reducing opioid medications","Autotitrating positive airway pressure"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. In central sleep apnea, continuous positive airway pressure is considered first, and if it fails, adaptive servo-ventilation is next: it monitors breathing patterns in real time and delivers and adjusts pressure as needed. With normal heart function he lacks the reduced ejection fraction that makes it unsafe. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 26, 12, 33"},{"q":"A 50-year-old woman with a body mass index of 41 kg/m2 and obstructive sleep apnea is being evaluated, and her clinician's suspicion of obesity hypoventilation syndrome is low to moderate. Her serum bicarbonate is 31 mEq/L (normal 22-29). What is the next step?","choices":["Arterial blood gas","Repeat serum bicarbonate","Home sleep apnea test","Diagnose the syndrome now"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. When suspicion is low to moderate, a serum bicarbonate above 27 means a higher chance of obesity hypoventilation syndrome and the need for an arterial blood gas. Her 31 mEq/L crosses that line, and the gas measures the awake arterial carbon dioxide pressure the diagnosis requires (above 45 mm Hg). — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 32, 31, 8"},{"q":"A 47-year-old man with a body mass index of 38 kg/m2 has obstructive sleep apnea. Which additional finding, with no other cause found for it, should prompt screening for obesity hypoventilation syndrome?","choices":["A serum bicarbonate in the normal range","Loud snoring with witnessed apneas","Awake room-air saturation of 93%","Overnight lowest saturation of 86%"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Obese individuals, with or without obstructive sleep apnea, are screened when they have an unexplained awake room-air oxygen saturation of 94% or less or an overnight lowest under 80%, unexplained exertional dyspnea, signs of pulmonary hypertension or right heart failure, facial plethora or a raised bicarbonate. 93% awake qualifies. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 31, 30, 32"},{"q":"A 46-year-old man with moderate obstructive sleep apnea cannot tolerate continuous positive airway pressure despite several mask changes. Which option is described for his severity and his intolerance of the device?","choices":["Adaptive servo-ventilation","Modafinil","Radiofrequency ablation","Palatal implants"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Radiofrequency ablation is used in patients with mild to moderate obstructive sleep apnea who cannot tolerate continuous positive airway pressure. His disease is moderate and he cannot tolerate the device, which matches both conditions. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 11, 13, 26"},{"q":"A 50-year-old man started modafinil for residual daytime sleepiness from obstructive sleep apnea. Two weeks later he develops a widespread rash and feels unwell. Which adverse effect of the drug best fits?","choices":["Drug hypersensitivity reaction","Nervousness and anxiety","Worsening of his sleep apnea","Headache from the drug"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Listed side effects of modafinil and armodafinil include headaches, nervousness or anxiety, dizziness, nausea, and dermatologic and systemic hypersensitivities. A widespread rash with feeling unwell after starting the drug fits a dermatologic and systemic hypersensitivity reaction. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"A 49-year-old man with untreated obstructive sleep apnea drinks no alcohol. An abdominal ultrasound done for another reason shows hepatic steatosis, and he has no edema or jugular venous distension. Which comorbidity of his sleep disorder fits this finding?","choices":["Nonalcoholic fatty liver disease","Alcohol-related liver disease","Liver congestion from right heart failure","Type 2 diabetes mellitus"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Patients with obstructive sleep apnea have a 2 to 3 times higher prevalence of nonalcoholic fatty liver disease. Hepatic steatosis in a man who drinks no alcohol, with no signs of right heart failure, fits that comorbidity. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 20, 31"},{"q":"A 34-year-old woman with obstructive sleep apnea has seasonal allergic rhinitis, and the nasal congestion makes her positive airway pressure mask hard to use. Which treatment addresses her congestion?","choices":["Positional sleep shirt","A nasal steroid spray","Modafinil each morning","A sedative at bedtime"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Nasal steroids are used in patients with obstructive sleep apnea for nasal congestion and inflammation from allergic rhinitis. Treating her congestion targets the problem that is getting in the way of her positive airway pressure therapy. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 11, 12, 6, 15"},{"q":"A 66-year-old man with heart failure and chronic obstructive pulmonary disease has loud snoring, witnessed apneas and daytime sleepiness. His insurer suggests a home sleep apnea kit. Which test should be used instead?","choices":["Thyroid-stimulating hormone level","In-laboratory polysomnogram","Home sleep apnea kit at a later date","Positive airway pressure titration"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. At-home kits suit uncomplicated suspected obstructive sleep apnea, but in significant cardiopulmonary disease a polysomnogram is used, not an at-home kit. His heart failure and chronic lung disease are that comorbidity, and heart failure is also a risk factor for central apnea, which a home kit cannot assess. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 8, 24"},{"q":"A 54-year-old woman who has taken opioid medications for chronic back pain for years has snoring, frequent awakenings and daytime sleepiness. Which sleep study fits her?","choices":["Questionnaire score before any study","Positive airway pressure titration","Polysomnogram in a sleep laboratory","Home sleep apnea kit"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Long-term opioid use is one of the settings where a polysomnogram, not an at-home kit, is used. Opioid medications are also a risk factor for central sleep apnea, which home kits cannot assess, since they assess obstructive sleep apnea and not other sleep disorders. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 8, 16, 24, 26"},{"q":"A 47-year-old man has obstructive sleep apnea newly confirmed on polysomnogram, with daytime sleepiness and hypertension. Which treatment is the most effective option for him?","choices":["Nasal expiratory resistance device","Modafinil for daytime sleepiness","Oral appliance worn during sleep","Continuous positive airway pressure"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Continuous positive airway pressure is the most effective non-pharmacologic treatment for obstructive sleep apnea. It also improves consequences of untreated disease, such as reflux, raised proinflammatory cytokines and, in the elderly, drowsiness and nocturia. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 12, 11, 8, 19, 22"},{"q":"A 61-year-old man was diagnosed with obstructive sleep apnea on a sleep study at another center and now needs the pressure on his new device set. Which study is used?","choices":["Split-night polysomnogram","Overnight diagnostic polysomnogram","Home sleep apnea kit","Pressure titration study"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A positive airway pressure titration is used when the diagnosis of sleep-disordered breathing is already known; it finds the exact air pressure needed to keep his airway open. His diagnosis is established, so a diagnostic study is not repeated first. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"A 59-year-old man with untreated obstructive sleep apnea declines treatment and asks how the condition affects his heart. Which risk can be raised as much as 6 times in untreated disease?","choices":["Driving accidents","Sudden cardiac death at night","Sudden cardiac death during exercise","Hypertension"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Untreated obstructive sleep apnea carries a 3- to 6-fold risk of sudden cardiac death at night, alongside high mortality from heart failure. The timing is the point: the excess risk falls during sleep, when the apneas occur. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 18, 15"},{"q":"A 59-year-old woman with a neuromuscular disease causing generalized weakness has snoring and morning headaches. Her awake room-air oxygen saturation is 92% (normal 95-100%). Which study should evaluate her for sleep-disordered breathing?","choices":["Home sleep apnea kit","Repeat saturation in a month","Pressure titration study","Attended polysomnogram"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Neuromuscular weakness and awake hypoventilation or hypoxemia are both conditions in which a polysomnogram is used instead of an at-home kit. Her room-air saturation of 92% is below normal, and a polysomnogram can also find sleep disorders other than obstructive sleep apnea. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 8, 31"},{"q":"A 58-year-old woman with obesity hypoventilation syndrome stopped her positive airway pressure therapy a year ago. She now has exertional dyspnea, an elevated jugular venous pressure, hepatomegaly and pedal edema. Which complication has developed?","choices":["Central sleep apnea","Insulin resistance","Right-sided heart failure","Nonalcoholic fatty liver disease"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Obesity hypoventilation syndrome tends to progress if left untreated, and many patients develop cardiovascular complications, pulmonary hypertension and right-sided heart failure. Elevated jugular venous pressure, hepatomegaly and pedal edema are the listed signs of right-sided heart failure. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 35, 34, 31"},{"q":"A 52-year-old woman with untreated obstructive sleep apnea has nightly heartburn and symptoms of laryngopharyngeal reflux. Which treatment of her sleep apnea is expected to improve her reflux?","choices":["Nasal expiratory resistance device","Positional therapy with a sleep belt","Nasal steroid for congestion","Continuous positive airway pressure"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. 75 to 100% of patients with untreated obstructive sleep apnea have gastroesophageal reflux disease or laryngopharyngeal reflux, driven by decreased intrathoracic pressure, and the reflux improves with continuous positive airway pressure. Treating her apnea treats a cause of her reflux. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 19, 12, 11"}]};
